            "text": "ROOT CAUSE:\nThe loop condition `i < n` terminates the loop before adding `n` to `sum`, leading to an off-by-one error where the last number is omitted.\n\nBEGINNER EXPLANATION:\nThe `for` loop uses the condition `i < n`, which stops executing as soon as `i` reaches `n`. Because of this, `n` is never added to `sum`. For example, when `n` is 5, it only adds 1, 2, 3, and 4. Changing the loop condition to `i <= n` ensures that `n` is included in the total.\n\nPATCHED CODE:\n```java\nimport java.util.Scanner;\npublic class J06 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            sum += i;\n        }\n        System.out.println(sum);\n    }\n}\n```",
